---
import LaunchLayout from "../layouts/LaunchLayout.astro";
import { PILOT_PRICE, REPOSITORY } from "../config/launch";
---
<LaunchLayout title="Investor brief | Velvet" description="Velvet's product thesis, implemented capabilities, commercial entry point, evidence boundaries, and milestones for pre-seed conversations.">
  <div class="l-wrap l-page"><div class="l-page-head"><p class="l-eyebrow">Investor brief / Coriolis Labs</p><h1>The control plane<br />for consequential<br />agent actions.</h1><p class="l-lede">The product thesis: turn business constraints into testable, enforceable action contracts with evidence that survives outside the agent and its gateway.</p><div class="l-actions"><a class="l-button" href="/contact/?intent=investor">Start a pre-seed conversation ↗</a><a class="l-button l-button-quiet" href="/evidence/">Inspect the evidence first</a></div></div>
    <div class="l-callout"><strong>Available today</strong><p>Today, Velvet is local, self-hosted code with assisted evaluation and supported execution boundaries. The next step is to make the same business-action contracts reusable across customer workflows. A hosted control plane remains on the roadmap.</p></div>
    <section class="l-page-section l-split"><div><h2>The problem</h2><p class="l-lede">Permission to call a tool does not, by itself, establish that a business constraint held. The same state change may have other routes, and the permitted action can still differ from the approved operation.</p></div><div><h2>The approach</h2><p class="l-lede">Define the outcome. Assess declared routes. Enforce supported operations at the relevant execution boundary. Reconcile state and retain evidence. Rerun the same checks when the workflow changes.</p></div></section>
    <section class="l-page-section"><h2>What exists today</h2><div class="l-table-wrap"><table class="l-table"><thead><tr><th scope="col">Surface</th><th scope="col">Implemented entry point</th><th scope="col">Important boundary</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ShadowPath</td><td>Effect projects, isolated route trials, observations, and portfolio results</td><td>Coverage is declared, not inferred for unknown routes.</td></tr><tr><td>Gateway &amp; permits</td><td>Typed pre-dispatch decisions and exact-request permit binding</td><td>Only supported, integrated execution paths are controlled.</td></tr><tr><td>Vault &amp; verification</td><td>Tamper-evident records and independently runnable verification</td><td>A receipt is not itself proof of an external business effect.</td></tr><tr><td>Protected refunds</td><td>Exact authorization, budget and operation identity in one PostgreSQL transaction</td><td>A local reference ledger, not external payment settlement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p class="l-fine">Sources: <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md`}>implementation status</a>, <a href="/evidence/">pinned evidence</a>, and <a href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/docs/public/protected-refunds.md`}>the refund contract</a>.</p></section>
    <section class="l-page-section l-split"><div><h2>The first sale</h2><p>Start with a paid assessment of one consequential staging workflow. The indicative starting offer is {PILOT_PRICE}. The customer keeps the rerunnable project and sees which work is needed to improve the boundary.</p><p>The proposed expansion is maintained regression coverage, additional workflows, and supported enforcement integrations. Recurring packaging and willingness to pay still need customer validation.</p><a class="l-inline-link" href="/pilot/">Review the exact starting offer ↗</a></div><div><h2>Why it could compound</h2><p>The reuse hypothesis is that each deployment improves a library of business constraints, adapters, observations, and regression evidence. That must be demonstrated through lower integration effort and repeated use across customers.</p><p>A longer list of findings is not, on its own, a durable advantage. The question is whether the next customer can adopt a useful contract faster.</p></div></section>
    <section class="l-page-section"><h2>What the next capital should establish</h2><div class="l-grid"><article class="l-card"><span class="l-step">01 / CUSTOMER VALIDATION</span><h3>Paid, bounded deployments</h3><p>Identify the workflow, buying owner, acceptance criteria, and evidence that changes a release decision. Validate this with paid customer engagements.</p></article><article class="l-card"><span class="l-step">02 / PRODUCT REUSE</span><h3>Repeatable integration</h3><p>Measure the work needed to deploy the same contract for a second customer. Build documentation, adapters, and failure handling around actual usage.</p></article><article class="l-card"><span class="l-step">03 / RECURRING VALUE</span><h3>Customer-operated checks</h3><p>Show that a customer's engineer can rerun the checks after a change and has a reason to pay for ongoing coverage.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="l-page-section l-prose"><h2>Meet the builder</h2><p>Paul Chumbe is building Velvet at Coriolis Labs. Start with the public source, recorded references, and implementation status. Customer adoption and recurring packaging are the next commercial milestones.</p><p>Discuss company ownership, cap table, budget, financing terms, and any permissioned private evidence directly in a scoped diligence exchange.</p><a class="l-inline-link" href={`${REPOSITORY}/blob/main/docs/public/investor-brief.md`}>Read the repository investor memo ↗</a></section>
    <div class="l-actions"><a class="l-button" href="/contact/?intent=investor">Talk with Paul ↗</a><a class="l-button l-button-quiet" href="/trust/">Review current limitations</a></div>
  </div>
</LaunchLayout>
